    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "595c21ddcf1047b96f196e6de5d1b25e27879a15",
            "parents": [
                "9e88729e407ad3dd150fca8b3da5ef9f62f45b11"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/63/87963/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Arya K Padman",
                "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                "username": "aryakpadman"
            },
            "createdOn": 1772511464,
            "author": {
                "name": "Arya K Padman",
                "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                "username": "aryakpadman"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why did we choose to use a signal instead of sd_notify or a dbus request?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, using `sd_notify()` or a `D-Bus` method would allow implementing reload in a synchronous manner. However, at present, I couldn\u2019t find any use case that requires synchronous reload .\n\nRegarding `sd_notify()`, adopting it would require changing the service from `Type=dbus` to either `Type=notify or Type=notify-reload`, since systemd does not support multiple Type= modes. Switching away from the current type Type=dbus would alter how readiness is tracked. Currently, systemd considers the service ready once the D-Bus name is acquired. With `Type=notify/Type=notify-reload`, we would need to explicitly send `sd_notify(\"READY=1\")` after acquiring the D-Bus name.Also, Type=dbus implicitly manages D-Bus-related ordering and dependencies. Moving away from it would require explicitly specifying those dependencies in the service file.\n\nAnd regarding `D-Bus-based mechanism`, we currently do not have an PDI to implement reload mechanism and hence I had proposed a PDI for the same sometime back and the feedback was since no synchronous reload is required, and systemd-based signaling satisfies the current need, using a signal-based reload is simple and sufficient. Attaching reference for the PDI patch : https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/+/83048\n\nBased on these considerations, the SIGHUP based approach was chosen."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Marking as resolved."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Please don't code-escape URLs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "ok will update."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "Why is comment is removed?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Brought back."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "With requesting the name now being done after the Manager is created, you could be changing the d-bus signals emitted on startup, which some applications could be depending on to work properly."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Yeah agree, Will update.\nThanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Hold up... requesting the name after the manager is created actually the _correct_ behavior.  You're not suppose to emit \"InterfacesAdded\" signals on startup."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Sure, but I'm not OK with silently breaking people and not finding out about it until I hear about it in a customer's data center.  So at the very least some due diligence would need to be done."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Also, I think changing the behavior would be outside the scope of this commit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Since settingsd can potentially create _any_ object in various machine-specific YAML deployments, I'm not sure how we could ever test that we wouldn't break someone.  Do you have a proposed way to test that?  What kind of due-diligence are you looking for?\n\nI'm not of the opinion we should stop making changes because it _might_ break someone; especially changes that are moving the codebase towards more-correctness.  That's what testing is for."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Was thinking do a search on xyz.openbmc_project.Settings and then see if the code that uses it is involved in interfacesAdded watches.  And if it is.... I don't know what to do, check which service starts first and go from there I guess."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "We can double check if any processes are doing watches on the xyz.openbmc_project.Settings bus name (which I hope they're not) but I don't think that provides much coverage on the concern.\n\nSince settingsd can implement any dbus object defined in phosphor-dbus-interfaces, someone could do something crazy like have a YAML file that implements xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.NetworkInterface, which phosphor-networkd is watching for MAC address changes.  I don't know how we check every daemon for \"are they doing the right set of handling not only InterfaceAdded but also NameAcquired (in order to follow up with mapper(?) to see if new objects they're trying to observe have shown up)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "As far as I can tell, the only dbus-watch being done explicitly on `xyz.openbmc_project.Settings` is one in x86-power-control.  This one is wrong, in this regard, but also probably not likely to be fundamentally broken because it is just trying to observe property changes.  You'd have to change the setting (manually) while the settingsd isn't running in order to have the bug exhibited."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I just remembered phosphor-logging, I've had code for over 6 years now that tries a settings read and adds IA and PC changes, and since phosphor-logging is dbus activated I imagine it can run before settings is up.\n\nin hindsight I wish I never used settings for anything and just hosted it myself, but alas."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "I agree with Patrick that we should not emit a signal during restore, but we do need to emit it during reload.\n\nThat said, the restore fix is outside the scope of this commit. I\u2019d prefer to address it in a separate commit and handle any dependent services there if needed. Any thoughts?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "I understand the concerns raised. This seems to require further discussion before making a decision.Also, bus name acquisition is not strictly part of this change. I will split this out into a separate commit and keep the bus name acquisition unchanged in this patch. We can then discuss and address it independently.\n\nI pushed the separate commit for the same here[https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-settingsd/+/88601]"
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_main.cpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_manager.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 203,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "During reload we need to emit the signal, right? are we emitting?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "settings_manager.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 203,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arya K Padman",
                        "email": "aryakpadman@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aryakpadman"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, it will emit property changed signals after updates the value into DBus and is handled by the DBus generated code to set the value."
                }
            ],
